    fix(sim): prevent TotalOutputTokens double-count after preemption (#1092)
    
    * fix(sim): move TotalOutputTokens accounting to completion time to prevent double-counting after preemption
    
    Closes #1091
    
    When a request is preempted, its ProgressIndex is reset to 0. Previously,
    TotalOutputTokens was incremented inline for every decode step, so the tokens
    generated before preemption were counted again after the request re-ran from
    scratch. Fix: remove the inline TotalOutputTokens++ and instead compute
    decode tokens once at completion time using PI_final - len(InputTokens),
    which is correct regardless of preemption history.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * fix(sim): update stale comment after TotalOutputTokens moved to completion time
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * fix(sim): correct test eviction-trigger comment and clarify decode-token formula
    
    - Test comment incorrectly attributed the eviction to B needing a 4th
      block; the actual trigger is A needing a new block for its first decode
      step (position 16 falls outside block3 which covers 0-15).
    - Clarify that PI - InputLen counts decode-step increments (= OutputLen - 1),
      not OutputLen, because the first output token is generated at prefill
      completion rather than as a decode-step increment.
    - Add inline note on the decodeTokens > 0 guard explaining the zero-output
      case.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * fix(sim): correct block layout and eviction trigger in test comment
    
    Previous attempt (3dabfa5) swapped the attribution but introduced a new
    error: the block layout still showed B holding 3 blocks (2 input + 1 decode)
    and A holding 1 input block, with eviction incorrectly attributed to A.
    
    Correct sequence: B gets block0 (input 0-15) and block2 (input 16-31); A
    gets block1 (input 0-15) and block3 (decode-0). B triggers eviction when it
    needs its first decode token (step 3) and all 4 blocks are occupied by B(2)+A(2).
    A is the victim (batch tail). Comment-only change; test logic unchanged.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
